+# Move demo videos to Cloudflare R2 + CDN
+
+## Request
+Implement `CLOUDFLARE_R2_CDN_VIDEO_IMPLEMENTATION.md` — migrate `public/Rehab.mp4`, `public/demos/education-demo.mp4`, and `public/demos/government-demo.mp4` to Cloudflare R2 behind a CDN domain, and add an ASP.NET upload path for future videos — then package the result for integration.
+
+## Approved plan
+See [plans/2026-08-15-cloudflare-r2-cdn-video.md](../plans/2026-08-15-cloudflare-r2-cdn-video.md).
+
+## Files changed
+- `lib/cdn.ts` (new) — `cdn(path)` resolves against `NEXT_PUBLIC_CDN_BASE_URL`, returning the bare path when unset. Also exports `videoAssets`, a map of the three canonical `videos/*` keys.
+- `lib/use-cases.ts` — education `demoVideo.src` → `videoAssets.educationDemo`.
+- `components/government-question-prompts.tsx` — government demo `<source src>` → `videoAssets.governmentDemo`.
+- `components/human-delivery.tsx` — rehab `<source src>` → `videoAssets.rehab`.
+- `tests/cdn.test.ts` (new) — 6 tests: absolute-URL and local-fallback branches, leading-slash normalization, no double slash, all keys under `videos/`, keys distinct.
+- `scripts/migrate-videos-to-r2.sh` (new) — one-time upload to stable `videos/*` keys via AWS CLI v2, then verification. `--verify` re-checks without re-uploading.
+- `.env.example` (new) — `NEXT_PUBLIC_CDN_BASE_URL`, with a note that R2 secrets must never take a `NEXT_PUBLIC_` prefix.
+- `.gitignore` — `public/**/*.{mp4,mov,webm}`, `.DS_Store`, and a `!.env.example` exception.
+
+The `<video>` elements are untouched, as the guide predicts — the browser does not care whether `src` is a path or an absolute URL.
+
+## Tests / checks run
+- `npm test` — 63 pass, 0 fail (6 new).
+- `npx tsc --noEmit` — 0 errors. (Required `npm ci` first; the checkout had no `node_modules`, which made every type in the repo appear unresolved.)
+- `npm run build` with `NEXT_PUBLIC_CDN_BASE_URL=https://assets.myos.ai` — succeeds, 15/15 static pages.
+- Grepped `.next/` output: the three `/videos/*.mp4` keys and the CDN host are present; **no** stale `/Rehab.mp4` or `/demos/*-demo.mp4` references remain.
+- `bash -n` on the migration script; its network paths are unexercised (no R2 credentials in session).
+- Patches verified by applying them to a fresh clone at `ef96a1b` and re-running the suite there: applies cleanly, 63 pass.
+
+## Deviations from plan
+- **Three call sites, not one.** The guide's §14 names only `lib/use-cases.ts`. `components/human-delivery.tsx` (`/Rehab.mp4`) and `components/government-question-prompts.tsx` (`/demos/government-demo.mp4`) also hardcode paths; migrating only `use-cases.ts` would have left two videos 404-ing after cleanup.
+- **Added `videoAssets`** rather than calling `cdn()` at each site, so the three keys cannot drift and a re-encode is a one-line change.
+- **Backend needs request-size limits the guide omits.** ASP.NET's default multipart limit is ~128 MB and Kestrel's default request body limit is 30 MB, so a 250 MB video is rejected before the controller runs. `FormOptions.MultipartBodyLengthLimit` and `Kestrel.Limits.MaxRequestBodySize` must both be raised, plus the IIS/Nginx equivalents.
+- **Added magic-byte validation** to the upload endpoint (MP4/MOV `ftyp` at offset 4, WebM EBML). The guide's MIME + extension checks both trust client-supplied strings; §20 recommends signature checking and it is cheap.
+- **Uploads use `TransferUtility`** (multipart above 8 MiB) so a 250 MB video is never buffered whole. The guide defers multipart to §22, but this comes free from the SDK.
+- **Backend code is not in this repo.** Per guide §6 it ships to the ASP.NET repo instead — delivered in the integration ZIP alongside a `Program.cs` snippet, `appsettings.sample.json`, `.env.example`, and the Cloudflare CORS policy.
+
+## Result
+Frontend migration is complete and verified in-repo: every video reference resolves through the CDN helper, the build emits CDN URLs, and nothing points at a local MP4. The site still works with the variable unset (paths fall back to `public/`), so this commit is safe to land ahead of the Cloudflare work.
+
+## Known limitations
+- **The videos are not in R2 and the CDN domain does not exist yet.** Both need Cloudflare account credentials. Until `NEXT_PUBLIC_CDN_BASE_URL` is set *and* the objects are uploaded, the site serves the local copies exactly as before — the change is inert, not broken.
+- **The three MP4s are still in `public/`.** Deliberate: the first deploy changes only where videos are served from, preserving a revert path. Cleanup is a later, separate commit.
+- **`git rm` will not shrink the repository** — the blobs stay in history; see the plan's risks.
+- **The backend code is written but never compiled.** No ASP.NET project was attached to this session, so it is reviewed source, not build-verified. `UploadController.cs` is a merge into the existing controller, not a drop-in file, and needs AWSSDK.S3 ≥ 3.7.400 for the checksum settings R2 requires.
+- **These commits were never pushed.** This session had read-only GitHub access — `git push` returned 403 at the egress proxy and the GitHub API returned `403 Resource not accessible by integration`. The commits exist locally and as patches in the integration ZIP; pushing needs `contents: write` on the repo grant.
+- Poster images and the rest of `public/` are untouched; moving them is guide §16, deferred.
