+# Move demo videos to Cloudflare R2 + CDN
+
+## Goal
+Get the three large MP4s out of the repo and serve them from Cloudflare R2 behind a custom CDN domain, so `public/` stops carrying ~135 MB of binary and video delivery gets edge caching and range requests. The frontend must only ever know public CDN URLs; all R2 write credentials stay server-side. Add an ASP.NET upload path so future videos can be published without another manual migration.
+
+## Assumptions (from the implementation guide)
+- CDN hostname is `https://assets.myos.ai`. Substitute everywhere if a different host is chosen — it appears in `.env.example`, the backend config, and the CORS policy.
+- Cloudflare account, R2 bucket, and custom domain are provisioned by the operator; nothing in this repo can create them.
+- Backend lives in a separate repository (the `CalmNguyen/api-retail-e-commerce` pattern). Per §6 of the guide, no C# is added to this repo — the R2 service and upload controller ship separately to that repo.
+- Object keys are versioned by rename (`rehab.mp4` → `rehab-v2.mp4`), never overwritten, so the edge can cache indefinitely.
+
+## Files likely to change
+- `lib/cdn.ts` (new) — `cdn()` resolving against `NEXT_PUBLIC_CDN_BASE_URL`, plus a `videoAssets` map of the three canonical keys.
+- `lib/use-cases.ts` — education `demoVideo.src` from a literal path to `videoAssets.educationDemo`.
+- `components/government-question-prompts.tsx` — government demo `<source src>`.
+- `components/human-delivery.tsx` — rehab clip `<source src>`.
+- `tests/cdn.test.ts` (new) — unit cover for path normalization and the configured/unconfigured branches.
+- `scripts/migrate-videos-to-r2.sh` (new) — one-time upload of the existing three files + public URL verification.
+- `.env.example` (new), `.gitignore` — CDN variable; block future MP4/MOV/WebM commits.
+
+Note: the guide's §14 only names `lib/use-cases.ts`, but **three** files hardcode video paths. Migrating only `use-cases.ts` would leave the government and rehab videos pointing at files that later cleanup deletes.
+
+## Ordered implementation steps
+1. Add `lib/cdn.ts` + `.env.example`; add `.gitignore` rules (including a `!.env.example` exception, since the existing `.env*` rule would swallow it).
+2. Switch all three video call sites to `videoAssets`.
+3. Add `scripts/migrate-videos-to-r2.sh` and `tests/cdn.test.ts`.
+4. **Operator — Cloudflare:** create bucket `myos-assets`; attach custom domain `assets.myos.ai` (not the rate-limited `*.r2.dev` endpoint); create an Object Read & Write API token scoped to the bucket; apply the CORS policy; optionally add a one-year edge cache rule for `assets.myos.ai/videos/*`.
+5. **Operator — upload:** run `scripts/migrate-videos-to-r2.sh` with `R2_ACCOUNT_ID`, `R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID`, `R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY`, `R2_BUCKET`, `CDN_BASE_URL` set. Requires AWS CLI v2.
+6. **Operator — deploy:** set `NEXT_PUBLIC_CDN_BASE_URL` in the hosting provider for Production *and* Preview, then deploy.
+7. **Cleanup, days later:** `git rm` the three MP4s once CDN playback is confirmed in production.
+8. **Backend, independent:** add the R2 service + video upload endpoint to the ASP.NET repo.
+
+Steps 4–6 are gated on credentials and cannot be done from this repo.
+
+## Validation method
+- `npm test` and `npx tsc --noEmit` for the helper and call sites.
+- `npm run build` with `NEXT_PUBLIC_CDN_BASE_URL` set, then grep `.next/` to confirm no stale `public/` MP4 paths survive in the output.
+- The migration script self-verifies each public URL on three axes and fails loudly unless all pass: `HEAD` returns 200; a ranged `GET` returns 206 (without this, seeking forces a full download); `Content-Type` is `video/mp4` (a wrong type is the usual cause of a black `<video>` box).
+- In-browser after deploy: posters render, scrubbing works both directions, mobile Safari plays, no mixed-content/CORS/404/403 in devtools.
+
+## Risks / unresolved decisions
+- **No rollback if cleanup is rushed.** The first deploy changes only where videos are *served from*; the local MP4s stay so reverting the env var restores a working site. Deleting them in the same change removes that escape hatch. Cleanup is deliberately a separate, later step.
+- **`git rm` does not shrink the repository.** The blobs remain in history and every clone still pays for them. Reclaiming that needs `git filter-repo` and a force-push rewriting every collaborator's history — a separate coordinated operation, worth it only if clone size becomes a real problem.
+- **Production must always set `NEXT_PUBLIC_CDN_BASE_URL`.** The unset-variable fallback returns bare paths, which is a local-development convenience only; in production it would 404 on `/videos/*` paths that never existed under `public/`.
+- **Poster images stay local for now** (guide §16 lists them as an optional later step). `cdn()` is ready for them.
+- Backend request-size limits are an unstated prerequisite the guide omits — see the log.
